Note: Entity Framework matches table names in the database with DbSet property name of Modal class in DbContext class, rather than with the names of the corresponding model classes
.
public DbSet<AAA> Student { get; set; } => Here AAA is Modal class and Student is DbSet property for AAA Modal class. Here it will AutoBind to Student table in Db because DbSet Property matches with tablename

By default, Entity Framework follows a set of conventions, and one of these conventions is the naming convention for DbSet properties in your DbContext class. 
The convention is to name DbSet properties in plural form and Pascal Case. Entity Framework then assumes that the table name in the database is the pluralized 
version of the DbSet property name.

For example, if you have a DbSet property named Students, Entity Framework will look for a table named Students in the database.
However, you can override this convention by specifying the table name explicitly using data annotations or the fluent API in the OnModelCreating method (See below i changed the property to BBB),
 if you need to deviate from the default behavior.


