Yes, you can definitely use both approaches in the same project. The "Database First" and "Code First" approaches in Entity Framework are not mutually exclusive.

If you originally started with a "Database First" approach and generated your models from an existing database, and now you want to add a new table to the database, you can still use "Code First" for that new table.

Here's how you can do it:

Create a new model class: Define a new class in your project that represents the structure of the new table you want to add.

Define the DbContext: If you haven't already, create a class that inherits from DbContext and add a DbSet property for your new model class.

Enable migrations: If you haven't already enabled migrations in your project, you'll need to do that. Run the Enable-Migrations command in the Package Manager Console.

Add a migration: After enabling migrations, you can add a new migration using the Add-Migration command. This will generate the necessary code to update the database schema to include the new table.

Apply the migration: Finally, you can apply the migration to update the database by running the Update-Database command.

By following these steps, you can seamlessly integrate "Code First" for the new table while still maintaining the existing "Database First" approach for the rest of your project.
